Agent: That’s a meaningful project — and there are several good avenues you can follow to learn more about a WWII‑era German refugee’s journey to the U.S. Below I’ve laid out (A) practical research steps you can take, (B) the key repositories and databases to check, and (C) how to request records and what to do next.

A — First steps (gather what you already know)
- Collect everything your family already has: full name (including any alternate spellings), birth date/place, parents’ names, approximate immigration year, arrival port or U.S. city, naturalization date/place, Social Security number, marriage/death records, old letters, photos, diaries, or newspaper clippings.
- Talk with relatives (even distant ones) — oral memory often supplies arrival years, sponsors’ names, or the U.S. town of first residence.
- Make a timeline of known events; that will help target searches.

B — Key places to search and what they hold
1. USCIS Genealogy Program (U.S. Citizenship & Immigration Services)
   - Holds historical immigration and naturalization files (A‑files) for many people who became permanent residents or citizens. Useful if your great‑aunt naturalized or had an alien file in the U.S.
2. NARA — National Archives and Records Administration (U.S.)
   - Passenger lists/ship manifests (for arrivals by sea).
   - Naturalization records, border entry records.
   - Search the NARA catalog and ask about the regional Archives that cover your arrival city.
3. Arolsen Archives (formerly International Tracing Service)
   - Massive collection on displaced persons, DP camps, post‑war refugee records in Europe. Their online database and tracing requests can sometimes return camp registrations and transport lists.
4. U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum (Washington, D.C.)
   - Holocaust Survivors and Victims Database; also oral histories and references to DP movement. Even if not Holocaust‑related, they have useful DP/refugee resources.
5. Yad Vashem and other Holocaust/war archives (if relevant)
   - Central databases and name indices (useful if Jewish ancestry or Holocaust context).
6. FamilySearch / Ancestry / MyHeritage
   - Passenger lists, naturalization indexes, census records, and user‑shared family trees. Ancestry and MyHeritage have subscription paywalls but are rich sources.
7. Ellis Island / Castle Garden
   - Useful for pre‑1924 arrivals; post‑WWII refugees often arrived through different ports, but these are worth checking if arrival was earlier.
8. State & local records in the U.S.
   - County clerk, state archives for birth, marriage, death, and naturalization certificates; local newspapers (obituaries, arrival notices).
9. Church/parish records
   - If you know religion/denomination, parish records (baptism, marriage) in Germany or U.S. may help.
10. German archives (Bundesarchiv, local Standesamt)
   - For birth records, wartime records, local registrations in Germany (civil registration).
11. International Red Cross (or International Committee of the Red Cross)
   - Tracing service and records for displaced persons and family tracing.
12. DP/Refugee program histories
   - Background reading: Displaced Persons Act (1948), UNRRA/International Refugee Organization activities, resettlement programs in the U.S. — useful context when you find records.

C — How to request records and practical tips
- Start locally and in the U.S.: search census (1940, 1950), Social Security Death Index, local newspapers, and naturalization indexes. Those often give arrival year and port.
- Use the USCIS Genealogy web pages to submit a request if you suspect there is an A‑file or naturalization file.
- For NARA passenger lists, search the NARA catalog or the digitized passenger lists on Ancestry/FamilySearch.
- Use the Arolsen Archives online search and, if needed, submit a tracing request (they help with DP camp and migration records).
- When contacting archives, provide as much identifying detail as possible to speed searches (full name, DOB, place of birth, approximate arrival date, any known aliases).
- Expect waiting times — some agencies take weeks or months for responses. Many repositories require proof of relationship for access to certain files.
- Keep organized records of every request you make (date, contact, reference number).

D — Other research methods & next actions
- Search digitized newspapers (Chronicling America, local library databases) for arrival notices, interviews, obituaries.
- Look for city directories in the town where she first lived in the U.S.
- If she naturalized, order her naturalization certificate (if you can find the location/court) — it often lists arrival date, ship, and birthplace.
- Consider DNA testing (AncestryDNA, 23andMe) to connect with relatives in Germany or descendants of other refugees who may have family stories.
- If you want, share the basic identifying info you have (name, birth year/place, likely arrival period) and I can suggest targeted search terms and next queries you can run on the databases above.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a message or a list of questions for relatives to help collect oral-history details.
- Prepare an ordered checklist of records to request (what to request first and where).
- Suggest exact search queries (name formats, variant spellings, likely ports) you can paste into Ancestry/FamilySearch/NARA.

Which would be most helpful right now — a checklist of next record requests, or a draft family interview template to gather more details from relatives?
